  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.6 z²)/2, steeper along x (across the screen). The surface is drawn at half the loss for readability; the Loss readout gives the true value. Plain steps diverge along x for learning rates above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Each update: step = −rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) per axis, so it shrinks as batch b grows and is exactly zero at 64. It comes from a fixed seed, run number and step number.",
    "Purple arrow: the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Dark arrow: the step the full data would give. Both are drawn at 2.5× their true length (one scale).",
    "A run ends after 40 updates (finished), or as diverged when the loss passes 5 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. A diverged ball and its path are drawn at the rim. Status shows ready before the first update.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per update (up to 40 updates). The ghost keeps the last run's path. Changing a setting mid-run applies from the next update.",
    "One update happens every 4 host steps (about 7.5 per second at speed 1)."
  ]
}